	shorttitle = {Efficacy and safety of the {RTS},{S}/{AS01} malaria vaccine during 18 months after vaccination},
	doi = {10.1371/journal.pmed.1001685},
	abstract = {Background:A malaria vaccine could be an important addition to current control strategies. We report the safety and vaccine efficacy (VE) of the RTS,S/AS01 vaccine during 18 mo following vaccination at 11 African sites with varying malaria transmission.Methods and Findings:6,537 infants aged 6-12 wk and 8,923 children aged 5-17 mo were randomized to receive three doses of RTS,S/AS01 or comparator vaccine.VE against clinical malaria in children during the 18 mo after vaccine dose 3 (per protocol) was 46\% (95\% CI 42\% to 50\%) (range 40\% to 77\%; VE, p{\textless}0.01 across all sites). VE during the 20 mo after vaccine dose 1 (intention to treat [ITT]) was 45\% (95\% CI 41\% to 49\%). VE against severe malaria, malaria hospitalization, and all-cause hospitalization was 34\% (95\% CI 15\% to 48\%), 41\% (95\% CI 30\% to 50\%), and 19\% (95\% CI 11\% to 27\%), respectively (ITT).VE against clinical malaria in infants was 27\% (95\% CI 20\% to 32\%, per protocol; 27\% [95\% CI 21\% to 33\%], ITT), with no significant protection against severe malaria, malaria hospitalization, or all-cause hospitalization.Post-vaccination anti-circumsporozoite antibody geometric mean titer varied from 348 to 787 EU/ml across sites in children and from 117 to 335 EU/ml in infants (per protocol).VE waned over time in both age categories (Schoenfeld residuals p{\textless}0.001). The number of clinical and severe malaria cases averted per 1,000 children vaccinated ranged across sites from 37 to 2,365 and from -1 to 49, respectively; corresponding ranges among infants were -10 to 1,402 and -13 to 37, respectively (ITT). Meningitis was reported as a serious adverse event in 16/5,949 and 1/2,974 children and in 9/4,358 and 3/2,179 infants in the RTS,S/AS01 and control groups, respectively.Conclusions:RTS,S/AS01 prevented many cases of clinical and severe malaria over the 18 mo after vaccine dose 3, with the highest impact in areas with the greatest malaria incidence. VE was higher in children than in infants, but even at modest levels of VE, the number of malaria cases averted was substantial. RTS,S/AS01 could be an important addition to current malaria control in Africa.Trial registration:http://www.ClinicalTrials.gov NCT00866619. Please see later in the article for the Editors' Summary.},
	language = {en},
	number = {7},
	journal = {PLoS Medicine},
	author = {Agnandji, Selidji Todagbe and Lell, Bertrand and Fernandes, José Francisco and Abossolo, Béatrice Peggy and Kabwende, Anita Lumeka and Adegnika, Ayola Akim and Mordmüller, Benjamin and Issifou, Saadou and Kremsner, Peter Gottfried and Loembe, Marguerite Massinga and Sacarlal, Jahit and Aide, Pedro and Madrid, Lola and Lanaspa, Miguel and Mandjate, Sofia and Aponte, John J. and Bulo, Helder and Nhama, Abel and Macete, Eusébio and Alonso, Pedro and Abdulla, Salim and Salim, Nahya and Mtoro, Ali Takadir and Mutani, Paul and Tanner, Marcel and Mavere, Caroline and Mwangoka, Grace and Lweno, Omar and Juma, Omar Ali and Shekalaghe, Seif and Tinto, Halidou and D'Alessandro, Umberto and Sorgho, Hermann and Valea, Innocent and Ouédraogo, Jean Bosco and Lompo, Palpouguini and Diallo, Salou and Traore, Ousmane and Bassole, Armand and Dao, Edgard and Hamel, Mary J. and Kariuki, Simon and Oneko, Martina and Odero, Chris and Otieno, Kephas and Awino, Norbert and Muturi-Kioi, Vincent and Omoto, Jackton and Laserson, Kayla F. and Slutsker, Laurence and Otieno, Walter and Otieno, Lucas and Otsyula, Nekoye and Gondi, Stacey and Otieno, Allan and Ogutu, Bernhards and Ochola, Jew and Onyango, Irene and Oyieko, Janet and Njuguna, Patricia and Chilengi, Roma and Akoo, Pauline and Kerubo, Christine and Maingi, Charity and Olotu, Ally and Bejon, Philip and Marsh, Kevin and Mwabingu, Gabriel and Gitaka, Jesse and Owusu-Agyei, Seth and Asante, Kwaku Poku and Boahen, Owusu and Dosoo, David and Adjei, George and Adeniji, Elisha and Yawson, Abena Kunadu and Kayan, Kingsley and Chandramohan, Daniel and Greenwood, Brian and Lusingu, John and Gesase, Samwel and Malabeja, Anangisye and Abdul, Omari and Mahende, Coline and Liheluka, Edwin and Lemnge, Martha and Theander, Thor G. and Drakeley, Chris and Mbwana, Joyce and Ansong, Daniel and Agbenyega, Tsiri and Adjei, Samuel and Boateng, Harry Owusu and Rettig, Theresa and Bawa, John and Sylverken, Justice and Sambian, David and Sarfo, Anim